==Old English==

===Etymology===
The origin is disputed. A phonetically exact correspondence to {{cog|non|bjǫrn||bear}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*bernuz}} (more at {{m|gem-pro|*berô}}), but the English word is never used for "bear", while the Old Norse word is never used for "warrior". While it is not implausible that the term for a wild animal could become a poetic term for a warrior, Germanic scholars have been reluctant to accept the equation. Some considered it a variant of {{m|en|bairn||child, offspring", hence "boy, servant}}, and others derived it from a {{cog|gem-pro|*beron-||carrier}} (hence "servant; man, warrior", more at {{m|en|baron}}). Celtic origin has also been considered; thus, [[:w:John Rhys|Rhys]] took this to be a Germanic reflex of the Celtic title ''[[Brennus]]'', and [[:w:Henry Bradley|Bradley]] connected British ''[[Bernicia]]'' ({{cog|cy|brenhin}}, {{m|cy|brenin}}).

The word has the form ''biorn'' in early Old English attestations; it survives into early Middle English as ''beorn'' and takes the variant spellings ''bern, berne, burn, burne, bearn, bieren, beern, beerne'' in later Middle English. Middle English usage often interchanges it with [[baron]]. As ''berne'', it survives into the 16th century in Scottish dialect, but becomes indistinguishable from [[bairn]].

